1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,275
Here is an octagon on squared paper. A point hops around its edge, and every hop follows

2
00:00:06,275 --> 00:00:07,416
one simple rule.

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:11,934
After ninety two hops, it lands exactly where it started.

4
00:00:11,934 --> 00:00:16,316
Is that luck? Or does every path in this octagon come back?

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:22,303
Start with a ball on a pool table, leaving each cushion at the angle it arrived.

6
00:00:22,303 --> 00:00:27,726
Shoot at forty five degrees from here, and after fourteen bounces it is back where it

7
00:00:27,726 --> 00:00:29,512
began, heading the same way.

8
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:34,195
With a slope of root two, this rectangular path never repeats.

9
00:00:34,195 --> 00:00:39,015
So a simple rule can produce both repeating and non-repeating paths.

10
00:00:39,015 --> 00:00:43,552
Now change the rule: use parallel lines instead of equal angles.

11
00:00:44,433 --> 00:00:50,899
In 2018, Peter Albers and Serge Tabachnikov introduced a cousin of this game: symplectic

12
00:00:50,899 --> 00:00:53,985
billiards. No angles, only parallel lines.

13
00:00:54,266 --> 00:00:57,719
Take a rectangle four wide and three tall.

14
00:00:57,719 --> 00:01:04,954
Put a point, x zero, on the bottom at one, zero, and a second point, x one, on the right

15
00:01:04,954 --> 00:01:06,434
side at four, two.

16
00:01:06,733 --> 00:01:10,910
The rule for the next point: look at the side x one sits on.

17
00:01:10,910 --> 00:01:14,391
Draw a line through x zero, parallel to that side.

18
00:01:14,391 --> 00:01:17,941
Where it meets the edge again is x two: one, three.

19
00:01:18,233 --> 00:01:24,686
Repeat, reaching two steps back. For x three, go through x one, parallel to the top side,

20
00:01:24,686 --> 00:01:30,994
where x two sits: zero, two. For x four, through x two, parallel to the left side: back

21
00:01:30,994 --> 00:01:31,937
to one, zero.

22
00:01:32,233 --> 00:01:36,757
After four hops the path has closed up, and it repeats forever.

23
00:01:36,757 --> 00:01:41,353
That is called periodic. In a rectangle, every path is periodic.

24
00:01:41,633 --> 00:01:47,624
Notice: the even points, zero, two and four, form one chain, and the odd points another.

25
00:01:47,624 --> 00:01:51,233
Each even hop is steered by the odd point in between.

26
00:01:52,133 --> 00:01:55,594
Now the octagon. Take a five by six rectangle.

27
00:01:55,594 --> 00:02:01,989
Cut the bottom corners at forty five degrees, two squares in, and the top corners one

28
00:02:01,989 --> 00:02:02,741
square in.

29
00:02:03,033 --> 00:02:10,571
Every side is flat, upright, or at forty five degrees, on a line like x plus y equals

30
00:02:10,571 --> 00:02:14,118
ten. Every number on the right is whole.

31
00:02:14,118 --> 00:02:15,537
Hold on to that.

32
00:02:15,833 --> 00:02:19,115
The mirror line is x equals two and a half.

33
00:02:19,115 --> 00:02:25,603
Special octagons have grid corners, these four side directions, and a mirror parallel

34
00:02:25,603 --> 00:02:29,801
to a side. Turning or stretching them gives this shape.

35
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:38,055
In 2019, Albers, Banhatti, Sadlo, Schwartz and Tabachnikov ran computer experiments.

36
00:02:38,055 --> 00:02:44,712
Every path came back, with periods like sixteen, forty four and ninety two.

37
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,440
They conjectured that in every special octagon, every path is periodic, but could only

38
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:54,960
give computer evidence. This finding proves it.

39
00:02:55,833 --> 00:02:59,730
A corner sits on two sides, so the rule breaks there.

40
00:02:59,730 --> 00:03:06,126
From now on, every path means every path for which the rule stays defined, forwards and

41
00:03:06,126 --> 00:03:07,449
backwards forever.

42
00:03:07,733 --> 00:03:14,115
Corners also split paths. These two close points both head straight up, but the corner

43
00:03:14,115 --> 00:03:18,197
sends one to the top and the other to the slanted side.

44
00:03:18,466 --> 00:03:24,003
So corners are the troublemakers. Start at a corner, try every second point, and mark

45
00:03:24,003 --> 00:03:29,801
in red every even point you reach. This path leaves one corner and ends at another, forty

46
00:03:29,801 --> 00:03:30,778
six hops later.

47
00:03:31,066 --> 00:03:38,058
Do this for every corner and every choice, and this octagon has exactly twenty red dots.

48
00:03:38,058 --> 00:03:40,282
Twenty, not infinitely many.

49
00:03:41,166 --> 00:03:45,200
Why does that matter? Unroll the edge into a straight line.

50
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:47,934
The red dots chop it into twenty pieces.

51
00:03:48,233 --> 00:03:54,464
A moment of a path is two neighbouring points: where it is, and where it goes next.

52
00:03:54,464 --> 00:03:58,818
Plot the first across a square and the second up the side.

53
00:03:58,818 --> 00:04:00,545
Each moment is one dot.

54
00:04:00,833 --> 00:04:05,519
The red dots cut the square into fewer than four hundred usable boxes.

55
00:04:05,519 --> 00:04:11,678
Inside each box, no path ever hits a corner, and all starting pairs follow the same sequence

56
00:04:11,678 --> 00:04:12,281
of sides.

57
00:04:12,566 --> 00:04:15,998
Watch one box, from a path of period sixteen.

58
00:04:15,998 --> 00:04:19,658
Each hop carries the whole box onto another box.

59
00:04:19,658 --> 00:04:22,022
After sixteen hops, it is home.

60
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:27,272
In general, group starting pairs by their entire sequence of sides.

61
00:04:27,272 --> 00:04:33,729
Each such region contains a box. There are finitely many regions, so a region must come

62
00:04:33,729 --> 00:04:35,956
home. Every hop can be undone.

63
00:04:36,233 --> 00:04:40,257
Take two return laps to keep the two coordinates separate.

64
00:04:40,257 --> 00:04:46,155
Repeating forwards and backwards cannot stretch either coordinate without leaving the

65
00:04:46,155 --> 00:04:51,497
bounded table. Only flips remain, so four return laps bring every point home.

66
00:04:52,266 --> 00:04:57,051
So if there are finitely many red dots, every path is periodic.

67
00:04:57,051 --> 00:05:01,229
This criterion is due to Albers, Lander and Westermann.

68
00:05:01,229 --> 00:05:04,722
The question becomes: are the red dots finite?

69
00:05:05,596 --> 00:05:11,082
The obvious attempt: from the corners, slide in all four directions, again and again.

70
00:05:11,082 --> 00:05:15,148
That catches every red dot, so if it stopped, we would be done.

71
00:05:15,429 --> 00:05:20,946
The filled set has not closed after twenty thousand sampled points, with denominators

72
00:05:20,946 --> 00:05:24,385
up to sixteen thousand three hundred and eighty four.

73
00:05:24,385 --> 00:05:28,149
This does not prove it infinite. We need a different idea.

74
00:05:29,029 --> 00:05:34,911
Here is the new idea. Add the half grid: points whose coordinates are whole, or end in

75
00:05:34,911 --> 00:05:35,389
a half.

76
00:05:35,663 --> 00:05:38,701
On the edge, keep three kinds of dots.

77
00:05:38,701 --> 00:05:45,417
Green: whole number points. Blue: on a slanted side, both coordinates end in a half.

78
00:05:45,417 --> 00:05:49,175
Orange: on the top or bottom, x ends in a half.

79
00:05:49,463 --> 00:05:53,889
Sixteen green, six blue, four orange: twenty six dots.

80
00:05:53,889 --> 00:06:00,530
The key lemma: every even point of every path from a corner lands on one of them.

81
00:06:00,530 --> 00:06:03,071
Indeed, all twenty red dots do.

82
00:06:03,963 --> 00:06:11,240
Why? Each even hop slides in one of four directions, and each slide keeps one number

83
00:06:11,240 --> 00:06:18,171
fixed: upright keeps x, sideways keeps y, and on a slope, x minus y or x plus y.

84
00:06:18,463 --> 00:06:22,327
Follow the red path from the corner two, zero.

85
00:06:22,327 --> 00:06:28,963
It slides up a slope with x minus y equal to two, until it meets x equals five.

86
00:06:28,963 --> 00:06:31,231
So y is three: whole again.

87
00:06:31,529 --> 00:06:38,588
Sideways hops are special. Thanks to the mirror, a sideways hop lands on the mirror image:

88
00:06:38,588 --> 00:06:43,529
x becomes five minus x. Whole stays whole, and half stays half.

89
00:06:43,829 --> 00:06:50,309
Now the interesting moment. From zero, four, the slide keeps x plus y equal to four,

90
00:06:50,309 --> 00:06:53,703
and meets the side where x minus y is three.

91
00:06:53,703 --> 00:06:58,485
Add the equations and halve: x is seven halves, y is one half.

92
00:06:58,485 --> 00:06:59,333
A blue dot.

93
00:06:59,629 --> 00:07:05,455
Two whole numbers, added and halved, give a whole number or a half, never a quarter.

94
00:07:05,455 --> 00:07:10,933
Next, an upright hop keeps x at three and a half, up to the top: an orange dot.

95
00:07:11,229 --> 00:07:17,163
Orange dots are the danger. A slanted hop from here would lead to five, four and a half,

96
00:07:17,163 --> 00:07:21,141
and then to a point with quarters. The pattern would break.

97
00:07:21,429 --> 00:07:26,792
But it never happens. We reached the orange dot by an upright hop, steered by an odd

98
00:07:26,792 --> 00:07:32,155
point on an upright side. The next odd point comes from a sideways hop, a mirror, so

99
00:07:32,155 --> 00:07:34,389
it lands on the other upright side.

100
00:07:34,663 --> 00:07:39,303
So the next even hop is upright again, and x stays at three and a half.

101
00:07:39,303 --> 00:07:41,983
The mirror blocks the one dangerous move.

102
00:07:42,863 --> 00:07:46,681
Here, even points from corners stay on twenty six dots.

103
00:07:46,681 --> 00:07:51,611
Other special octagons have different counts, but always finitely many.

104
00:07:51,611 --> 00:07:54,874
So every path defined for all time is periodic.

105
00:07:54,874 --> 00:07:56,471
The conjecture is true.

106
00:07:56,763 --> 00:08:01,833
Here, the formula guarantees repetition within two thousand six hundred hops.

107
00:08:01,833 --> 00:08:07,563
That is a loose bound. The largest period found in the reported samples was ninety two.

108
00:08:08,429 --> 00:08:14,052
Was the mirror really needed? Here is a lopsided octagon: corners on the grid, the same

109
00:08:14,052 --> 00:08:15,797
four directions, no mirror.

110
00:08:16,096 --> 00:08:22,048
It has a path of period six. Notice the thirds: this loop never touches the half grid.

111
00:08:22,329 --> 00:08:28,295
Nudge the first point along its side. Each lap doubles the nudge and flips it: a twenty

112
00:08:28,295 --> 00:08:33,849
fourth, minus a twelfth, a sixth, minus a third, and then the path is thrown off.

113
00:08:34,129 --> 00:08:40,639
Nudge the second point instead. Each lap halves the nudge and flips it: a quarter, minus

114
00:08:40,639 --> 00:08:44,041
an eighth, a sixteenth, minus a thirty second.

115
00:08:44,329 --> 00:08:49,264
This path approaches the loop forever but never reaches it, so never repeats.

116
00:08:49,264 --> 00:08:54,134
A suitable nudge also avoids corners forever backwards, as the paper proves.

117
00:08:54,134 --> 00:08:57,145
Not every path is periodic: the mirror matters.

118
00:08:58,029 --> 00:09:03,668
So what is established? The theorem has a short written proof, checked line by line in

119
00:09:03,668 --> 00:09:07,077
internal reviews, but not yet refereed by a journal.

120
00:09:07,363 --> 00:09:13,620
Two independent exact programs also checked the dot rule for all one thousand four hundred

121
00:09:13,620 --> 00:09:18,417
and eighty four allowed parameter tuples with entries up to fourteen.

122
00:09:18,417 --> 00:09:22,171
These include rescaled copies: a check, not the proof.

123
00:09:22,463 --> 00:09:27,139
Confidence in the proof is high; confidence in originality is more limited.

124
00:09:27,139 --> 00:09:32,438
The campaign search found no earlier proof of this family result, but a search cannot

125
00:09:32,438 --> 00:09:33,623
establish priority.

126
00:09:33,896 --> 00:09:39,404
The same argument settles the companion Hexhouse conjecture in the mirror symmetric case:

127
00:09:39,404 --> 00:09:44,912
a square with a symmetric roof. Stretched sideways, its roof turns to forty five degrees.

128
00:09:45,196 --> 00:09:51,513
Still open in the paper: lopsided Hexhouses, the Penthouse conjecture, and octagons whose

129
00:09:51,513 --> 00:09:58,042
sizes are in no whole number ratio. The general question here concerns symplectic billiards,

130
00:09:58,042 --> 00:10:00,100
with this parallel-line rule.

131
00:10:00,996 --> 00:10:06,724
To recap. One: each new point comes from two steps back, by a parallel line.

132
00:10:06,724 --> 00:10:11,547
Two: finitely many points from corners means every path repeats.

133
00:10:11,547 --> 00:10:17,652
Three: whole number sides and the mirror keep those points on finitely many dots.

134
00:10:17,929 --> 00:10:23,656
Still unknown here: whether lopsided Hexhouses always repeat, and whether every convex

135
00:10:23,656 --> 00:10:27,385
polygon has even one repeating symplectic billiard path.
